<body>
<h1>CSI Data Collection Using ESP32 — Complete Technical Installation &amp; Setup Guide</h1>
<p>This document provides a comprehensive, step-by-step technical guide for setting up, building, flashing, and running the <b>CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32</b> system from scratch on a <b>freshly formatted Windows machine</b> with no prior development tools (no C/C++ compilers, no Python, no Git, and no ESP-IDF installed).</p>
<hr/>
<h2>1. System Architecture &amp; Prerequisites</h2>
<p>The system consists of embedded firmware running on ESP32 microcontrollers and host-side Python applications for device role management, real-time visualization, and CSV data logging.</p>
<h3>1.1. Programming Languages Used</h3>
<table><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Role in the Project</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>C (ISO C11 / GNU11)</b></td><td>ESP32 embedded firmware (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">main/main.c</code>), FreeRTOS tasks, ESP-NOW protocol handling, Wi-Fi CSI capture callbacks, UART command parsing, and NVS flash storage.</td></tr><tr><td><b>C++ (GNU++23)</b></td><td>ESP-IDF internal components and runtime libraries.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Python (3.12)</b></td><td>Host applications: Central Control GUI (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code>), Real-Time Web Dashboard backend (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">web_app.py</code>), Standalone Log Viewer (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code>), and ESP-IDF build/flash tools (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esptool.py</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>HTML5 / CSS3 / JavaScript</b></td><td>Real-time interactive CSI visualization frontend (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">static/dashboard.html</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>CMake &amp; Batch / PowerShell</b></td><td>Build system configuration (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CMakeLists.txt</code>) and environment/launcher automation scripts (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">.bat</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">.ps1</code>).</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>1.2. Frameworks, Compilers &amp; Toolchain</h3>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Embedded Framework:</b> <b>Espressif ESP-IDF v5.5.5</b> (includes FreeRTOS, ESP-WiFi, ESP-NOW, LWIP TCP/IP stack, NVS Flash, and UART VFS drivers).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Installer / Version Manager:</b> <b>ESP-IDF Installation Manager CLI (`eim` v0.20.1+)</b>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>C/C++ Cross-Compiler Toolchain:</b> <b>Xtensa ESP32 ELF GCC 14.2.0</b> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">xtensa-esp-elf-gcc</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">xtensa-esp32-elf-g++</code>, release <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp-14.2.0_20260121</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Language Server / Static Analyzer:</b> <b>ESP-Clang</b> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp-19.1.2_20250312</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">clangd</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Build System Tools:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>CMake:</b> Build generator.</p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Ninja:</b> Fast parallel build executor.</p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>ccache:</b> Compiler cache to accelerate subsequent rebuilds to a few seconds.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Flashing &amp; Debugging Tools:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>esptool.py (v4.12+):</b> Flashes compiled binary images (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">bootloader.bin</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">partition-table.bin</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">project-name.bin</code>) to ESP32 SPI flash over USB-UART.</p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>OpenOCD-ESP32:</b> On-chip debugging support.</p>
<h3>1.3. Host Python Libraries (System Python Environment)</h3>
<p>While ESP-IDF creates its own isolated Python virtual environment (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\tools\python\v5.5.5\venv</code>) for firmware compilation, the host GUI/Web tools (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">web_app.py</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code>) run on the <b>system Python</b> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">py</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">python</code>) and require:</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pyserial</code>: Bidirectional USB-UART serial communication with ESP32 boards.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">flask</code>: HTTP/REST web server powering the real-time dashboard (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">http://localhost:5000</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">tkinter</code>: Standard Python GUI toolkit (used by <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">numpy</code> &amp; <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">matplotlib</code>: Numerical processing and plotting for CSI amplitude/phase analysis.</p>
<h3>1.4. Hardware &amp; Driver Requirements</h3>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>At least 2x ESP32 Development Boards</b> (e.g., ESP32-WROOM or ESP32-U with external antenna; one configured as <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code> and one or more configured as <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Data-Capable USB Cables</b> (Micro-USB or USB-C depending on the board; ensure they are not charge-only cables).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>USB-to-UART Bridge Drivers:</b> <b>Silicon Labs CP210x</b> or <b>CH340/CH341</b> Windows driver, depending on the USB-serial chip on your ESP32 board.</p>
<hr/>
<h2>2. Step-by-Step Installation Instructions (From a Clean Windows PC)</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Enable PowerShell Script Execution</h3>
<p>On a freshly installed Windows system, PowerShell blocks <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">.ps1</code> scripts by default. Because ESP-IDF uses a PowerShell profile script to activate its environment, open <b>PowerShell</b> and run:</p>
<pre>Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser -Force</pre>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 2: Install Python 3.12, Git, and Espressif Installation Manager (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">eim</code>)</h3>
<p>Use Windows Package Manager (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">winget</code>) to install the core prerequisites.</p>
<h4>2.1. Install Python 3.12</h4>
<pre>winget install --id Python.Python.3.12 -e --source winget --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements</pre>
<blockquote><p><b>Note:</b> After installation, restart your terminal (or refresh <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">PATH</code>) and verify that <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">python --version</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">py --version</code> return <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">Python 3.12.x</code>.</p></blockquote>
<h4>2.2. Install and Configure Git</h4>
<p>To install the standard Git for Windows package (will prompt for Administrator permission):</p>
<pre>winget install --id Git.Git -e --source winget --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements</pre>
<p><i>(Alternatively, if installing without Administrator privileges, download `MinGit` or `PortableGit` from GitHub, extract it to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Git`, and add `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Git\cmd` to your User `Path` environment variable.)</i></p>
<p>After installing Git, configure your global Git username and email address:</p>
<pre>git config --global user.name &quot;NanamiLawliet&quot;
git config --global user.email &quot;burakmentesss@gmail.com&quot;</pre>
<h4>2.3. Install Espressif Installation Manager CLI (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">eim</code>) &amp; USB-UART Drivers</h4>
<pre>winget install --id Espressif.EIM-CLI -e --source winget --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements</pre>
<p>To install the Silicon Labs <b>CP210x</b> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller</code>) and <b>CH340/CH341</b> USB-to-UART drivers on a fresh Windows system, open <b>PowerShell as Administrator</b> and run:</p>
<pre>eim install-drivers</pre>
<p><i>(Or download and install the driver packages directly via `pnputil` in an Administrator PowerShell):</i></p>
<pre>$driverDir = &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\drivers&quot;
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path &quot;$driverDir\CP210x&quot; | Out-Null
curl.exe -4 -L -o &quot;$driverDir\CP210x.zip&quot; &quot;https://dl.espressif.com/dl/idf-installer/CP210x_Universal_Windows_Driver.zip&quot;
Expand-Archive -Path &quot;$driverDir\CP210x.zip&quot; -DestinationPath &quot;$driverDir\CP210x&quot; -Force
pnputil /add-driver &quot;$driverDir\CP210x\silabser.inf&quot; /install</pre>
<h4>2.4. Refresh Environment Variables in the Current PowerShell Session</h4>
<p>To continue without restarting PowerShell:</p>
<pre>$env:Path = [System.Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;Path&quot;,&quot;Machine&quot;) + &quot;;&quot; + [System.Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;Path&quot;,&quot;User&quot;)</pre>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 3: Install Required Host Python Packages</h3>
<p>Install the Python libraries required by the Web Dashboard, Central Control GUI, and analysis scripts into the <b>system Python</b> environment:</p>
<pre>python -m pip install --upgrade pip flask pyserial numpy matplotlib</pre>
<blockquote><p><b>Warning — Do NOT run `pip install serial`:</b> The package name for serial port communication in Python is <b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pyserial</code></b> (which provides <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">import serial</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial.Serial</code>). There is an unrelated package on PyPI named <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial</code> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0.0.97</code>) that overwrites <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pyserial</code>&#x27;s <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial/__init__.py</code> and causes <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">AttributeError: module &#x27;serial&#x27; has no attribute &#x27;Serial&#x27;</code>. If you ever install <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial</code> by mistake, fix it with:</p><pre>python -m pip uninstall -y serial
python -m pip install --force-reinstall pyserial</pre></blockquote>
<p>Verify that all libraries (including <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">tkinter</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial.Serial</code>) are properly installed (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">ALL_IMPORTS_OK</code> should be printed):</p>
<pre>python -c &quot;import tkinter, serial, flask, numpy, matplotlib; assert hasattr(serial, &#x27;Serial&#x27;); print(&#x27;ALL_IMPORTS_OK&#x27;)&quot;</pre>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 4: Install ESP-IDF v5.5.5 and the C/C++ Toolchain (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">xtensa-esp-elf</code>)</h3>
<p>This project is built and tested against <b>ESP-IDF v5.5.5</b> targeting the <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp32</code> architecture. Run the following <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">eim</code> command to automatically download and configure ESP-IDF v5.5.5, the Xtensa GCC 14.2.0 C/C++ compiler, CMake, Ninja, ccache, and the isolated ESP-IDF Python virtual environment inside <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif</code>:</p>
<pre>eim install -n true -p &quot;C:\Espressif&quot; -i v5.5.5 -t esp32 --create-bat-activation-script true -v</pre>
<p>What this command does:</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>1.</b> Clones ESP-IDF <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">v5.5.5</code> and all required submodules into <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\v5.5.5\esp-idf</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>2.</b> Downloads and extracts <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">xtensa-esp-elf</code> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp-14.2.0_20260121</code>), <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp-clang</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">cmake</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">ninja</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">openocd-esp32</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf-exe</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">ccache</code>, and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp-rom-elfs</code> into <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\tools</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>3.</b> Creates the ESP-IDF Python virtual environment at <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\tools\python\v5.5.5\venv</code> and installs all IDF Python dependencies.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>4.</b> Generates the PowerShell activation script at <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\tools\Microsoft.v5.5.5.PowerShell_profile.ps1</code>.</p>
<blockquote><p><b>Optional Path Compatibility Junction:</b> If any legacy <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">.vscode</code> configuration references <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\frameworks\esp-idf-v5.5.5-2</code>, you can create a directory junction so both paths resolve identically:</p><pre>New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path &quot;C:\Espressif\frameworks&quot; | Out-Null
New-Item -ItemType Junction -Path &quot;C:\Espressif\frameworks\esp-idf-v5.5.5-2&quot; -Target &quot;C:\Espressif\v5.5.5\esp-idf&quot;</pre></blockquote>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 5: Install Visual Studio Code and Extensions</h3>
<p>Install Visual Studio Code (if not already installed) along with the official ESP-IDF, C/C++, and Python extensions:</p>
<pre># Install VS Code (if not already installed):
winget install --id Microsoft.VisualStudioCode -e --source winget --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements

# Install required VS Code extensions:
code --install-extension espressif.esp-idf-extension --force
code --install-extension ms-vscode.cpptools --force
code --install-extension ms-python.python --force</pre>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 6: Clone the GitHub Repository</h3>
<p>Create the project directory under <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">Desktop\apps\projects</code> and clone the repository:</p>
<pre>New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects&quot; | Out-Null
git clone &quot;https://github.com/NanamiLawliet/CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32.git&quot; &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects\CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32&quot;</pre>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 7: Build the ESP32 Firmware (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py build</code>)</h3>
<p>Navigate to the project directory, activate the ESP-IDF v5.5.5 environment, set the target to <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">esp32</code>, and compile the firmware:</p>
<pre>Set-Location &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects\CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32&quot;
. &quot;C:\Espressif\tools\Microsoft.v5.5.5.PowerShell_profile.ps1&quot;
idf.py set-target esp32
idf.py build</pre>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">sdkconfig.defaults</code> automatically enables Wi-Fi CSI (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_CSI_ENABLED=y</code>) in the generated <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">sdkconfig</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Once compilation succeeds, the following artifacts are generated inside <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/</code>:</p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/bootloader/bootloader.bin</code></p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/partition_table/partition-table.bin</code></p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/project-name.bin</code></p>
<p style="margin-left:38pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/compile_commands.json</code> (used by VS Code C/C++ IntelliSense)</p>
<hr/>
<h2>3. Flashing ESP32 Boards &amp; Running the System</h2>
<h3>Step 8: Flash the Generic Firmware to All ESP32 Boards</h3>
<p>This project uses a <b>single unified (generic) firmware image</b> for all ESP32 boards. Every board boots into <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">IDLE</code> mode by default until its role (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code> or <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code>) is dynamically assigned via the Central Control GUI and saved to non-volatile storage (NVS).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>1.</b> Plug your ESP32 boards into the computer via USB.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>2.</b> List the detected Serial COM ports:</p>
<pre>   py -c &quot;import serial.tools.list_ports; print(&#x27;\n&#x27;.join(f&#x27;{p.device}: {p.description}&#x27; for p in serial.tools.list_ports.comports()))&quot;</pre>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>3.</b> In an activated ESP-IDF PowerShell window (or using <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">5_Flash_ESP32.bat</code>), flash each board (e.g., <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">COM9</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">COM10</code>):</p>
<pre>   idf.py -p COM9 flash
   idf.py -p COM10 flash</pre>
<blockquote><p><b>Tip — First-Time Flash or When Switching SDK Versions (`erase-flash`):</b> Standard <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py flash</code> only overwrites the application partition (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x10000</code>) and leaves the non-volatile storage (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">nvs</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">phy_init</code> at <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x9000</code>–<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x10000</code>) untouched. If a board has stale or corrupted Wi-Fi / RF calibration data in NVS, it may boot normally and accept commands over USB Serial, but fail to transmit or receive any Wi-Fi packets (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">ESP-NOW status = FAIL</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">callbacks=0</code>). When flashing a board for the first time (or if no CSI data appears), perform a full flash erase + flash:</p><pre>idf.py -p COM9 erase-flash flash
idf.py -p COM10 erase-flash flash</pre><p><b>Important:</b> Do <b>NOT</b> leave <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py monitor</code> running after flashing. Only one program can hold a Windows COM port open at a time, and an active serial monitor will block <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">web_app.py</code> from connecting.</p></blockquote>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 9: Assign Device Roles Using the Central Control GUI</h3>
<p>Once the boards are flashed and connected via USB:</p>
<pre>cd &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects\CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32&quot;
py central_control.py</pre>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>1.</b> The GUI automatically scans all COM ports and displays each discovered ESP32 along with its MAC address, COM port, and current role (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">IDLE</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code>, or <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>2.</b> <b>Configure the Receiver Board:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Select your first ESP32 in the table.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Choose <b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code></b> from the <b>Role</b> dropdown.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Click <b>Apply Configuration</b>. The board saves the role to NVS and reboots into Receiver mode.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>3.</b> <b>Configure the Transmitter Board(s):</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Select your second ESP32 in the table.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Choose <b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code></b> from the <b>Role</b> dropdown.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Select the <b>Receiver&#x27;s MAC address</b> from the <b>Target Peer MAC</b> dropdown.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Select a <b>Transmitter ID</b> (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">1</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">2</code>, etc.).</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Click <b>Apply Configuration</b>. The board saves the settings to NVS and begins transmitting ESP-NOW frames to the Receiver.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>4.</b> Close the Central Control GUI before launching the Web Dashboard or Log Viewer so the serial ports are released.</p>
<hr/>
<h3>Step 10: Visualize and Record Real-Time CSI Data</h3>
<h4>Option A: Real-Time Web Dashboard (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">web_app.py</code>) — <i>Recommended</i></h4>
<pre>cd &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects\CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32&quot;
py web_app.py</pre>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Open <b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">http://localhost:5000</code></b> in your browser (Chrome recommended).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Select the <b>RECEIVER</b> board&#x27;s COM port from the top toolbar and click <b>Connect</b>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Observe real-time subcarrier amplitudes, RSSI, packet rate (Hz), and diagnostic counters.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Click <b>Record</b> to log incoming CSI frames to <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">sessions/session-&lt;id&gt;/csi_data_&lt;timestamp&gt;.csv</code>.</p>
<h4>Option B: Standalone Desktop CSI Log Viewer (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code>)</h4>
<pre>cd &quot;C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\Desktop\apps\projects\CSI-Data-Collection-Using-ESP32&quot;
py csi_log_viewer.py</pre>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> Connects directly to the Receiver&#x27;s serial port using a native Tkinter interface, writes session CSV files, and displays incoming CSI rows live without requiring a browser.</p>
<hr/>
<h2>4. Quick-Launch Scripts (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">Desktop\apps</code> Folder)</h2>
<p>For convenience, ready-to-use batch launchers are provided in the <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">Desktop\apps</code> directory:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Script Name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">1_Central_Control_GUI.bat</code></b></td><td>Launches <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code> to scan COM ports and assign <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code> / <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code> roles.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">2_Web_Dashboard.bat</code></b></td><td>Starts <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">web_app.py</code> and opens <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">http://localhost:5000</code> in the default web browser.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">3_CSI_Log_Viewer.bat</code></b></td><td>Launches the standalone desktop <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code> application.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">4_ESP_IDF_Terminal.bat</code></b></td><td>Opens an interactive shell (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">eim shell</code>) inside the project directory with ESP-IDF v5.5.5 activated.</td></tr><tr><td><b><code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">5_Flash_ESP32.bat</code></b></td><td>Lists available COM ports, prompts for a port name (e.g., <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">COM9</code>), and flashes the compiled firmware.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<hr/>
<h2>5. Troubleshooting</h2>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>1.</b> <b>`Missing Python package: flask` or `serial` when running `python web_app.py`:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> Running <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">python</code> inside an activated ESP-IDF terminal invokes the isolated ESP-IDF virtual environment (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">C:\Espressif\tools\python\...</code>), which does not have Flask installed.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Run <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">py web_app.py</code> (using the Windows Python Launcher) in a standard terminal, or double-click <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">2_Web_Dashboard.bat</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>2.</b> <b>`PermissionError(13, &#x27;Access is denied&#x27;)` on Serial Port:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> Another application (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py monitor</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code>, or another terminal) is currently holding the COM port open.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Close any other application using that COM port and click <b>Connect</b> again.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>3.</b> <b>No COM Ports Detected When ESP32 Is Plugged In:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> The USB cable is power-only (no data lines) or the USB-UART driver (CP210x / CH340) is missing.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Use a verified data-capable USB cable and install the Silicon Labs CP210x or CH340 driver (or run <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">eim install-drivers</code>).</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>4.</b> <b>Include Errors or Squiggly Lines in VS Code:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> The <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/compile_commands.json</code> file has not been generated yet.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Run <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py build</code> once so CMake generates <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">build/compile_commands.json</code>, then reload the VS Code window.</p>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>5.</b> <b>No CSI Data in `csi_log_viewer.py` / `web_app.py` (`ESP-NOW status = FAIL` on TX &amp; `callbacks=0` on RX):</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Symptom:</b> Both ESP32 boards are detected in <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code>, roles (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code>) apply and save properly, and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">csi_log_viewer.py</code> records a <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">.csv</code> file containing only the header row (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0 rows</code>). Checking serial logs shows <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">ESP-NOW status = FAIL</code> on the Transmitter and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">DIAG: CSI diag: callbacks=0 matches=0</code> on the Receiver—even after rebuilding and running <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py flash</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> Stale or corrupted Wi-Fi / PHY RF calibration data cached inside the ESP32&#x27;s NVS flash partition (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x9000</code>–<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x10000</code>). Because <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CONFIG_ESP_PHY_CALIBRATION_AND_DATA_STORAGE=y</code>, <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CONFIG_ESP_PHY_RF_CAL_PARTIAL=y</code>, and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_NVS_ENABLED=y</code> are enabled in <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">sdkconfig</code>, the ESP32 loads cached RF calibration parameters from NVS on boot. Because <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py flash</code> does not erase NVS, corrupted RF calibration data persists across reflashes and leaves the 2.4 GHz radio unable to transmit or receive frames.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Erase the NVS and PHY calibration region (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x9000</code> to <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">0x10000</code>) on <b>all</b> ESP32 boards (or run <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">idf.py -p COMx erase-flash flash</code>) so each board performs a fresh RF calibration on boot, then re-assign their roles in <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">central_control.py</code>:</p>
<pre>     # Option A: Erase only the NVS + PHY calibration region (fast, keeps firmware intact)
     &amp; &quot;C:\Espressif\tools\python\v5.5.5\venv\Scripts\python.exe&quot; -m esptool --chip esp32 -p COM9 erase_region 0x9000 0x7000
     &amp; &quot;C:\Espressif\tools\python\v5.5.5\venv\Scripts\python.exe&quot; -m esptool --chip esp32 -p COM11 erase_region 0x9000 0x7000

     # Option B: Full flash erase and re-flash inside an ESP-IDF terminal
     idf.py -p COM9 erase-flash flash
     idf.py -p COM11 erase-flash flash</pre>
<blockquote><p><b>Note:</b> Erasing NVS resets the boards back to <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">IDLE</code> role. Open <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">py central_control.py</code> afterwards and click <b>Apply Configuration</b> for your <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">RECEIVER</code> and <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">TRANSMITTER</code> boards.</p></blockquote>
<p style="margin-left:18pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>6.</b> <b>`AttributeError: module &#x27;serial&#x27; has no attribute &#x27;Serial&#x27;` When Connecting or Scanning Ports:</b></p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Cause:</b> Installing <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pip install serial</code> instead of <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pip install pyserial</code> installs an unrelated serialization package (<code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial 0.0.97</code>) that shadows <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pyserial</code> and removes <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial.Serial</code>.</p>
<p style="margin-left:48pt;text-indent:-12pt;"><b>•</b> <b>Solution:</b> Uninstall <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">serial</code> and force-reinstall <code style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;background-color:#f0f2f5;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:9.5pt;">pyserial</code>:</p>
<pre>     python -m pip uninstall -y serial
     python -m pip install --force-reinstall pyserial</pre>
</body></html>